<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III, Duke of Bourbon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1490">1490</a>–<a href="/years/1527">1527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Constable of Bourbon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, was Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the greatest noble of the kingdom. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> and his mother challenged his inheritance, he went over to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1523, and his lands were confiscated. He commanded imperial troops at Pavia and was killed leading the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1527, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="Charles III, Duke of Bourbon" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connetable_Bourbon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, born on 17 February 1490, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> de Bourbon, Count of Montpensier, and Clara Gonzaga, and belonged to a junior branch of the royal house. Both his parents were dead by 1503, and he was raised at the Bourbon court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a> beside his future wife, Suzanne de Bourbon, with a humanist and courtly education. His elder brother's death in 1501 made him Count of Montpensier.</p><p>On 26 February 1505 he contracted to marry Suzanne, heiress of the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> line, and after their marriage on 10 May 1505 he took the title of Duke of Bourbon. His domains included the duchies of Bourbon, Auvergne and Châtellerault and many counties, and he could raise soldiers and employ his own fiscal and judicial officers. The historian David-Chapy calls him the last feudatory able to oppose royal power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He fought for Louis XII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1507 and at Agnadello on 14 May 1509, was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> in 1512, and fought the Spanish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and the English in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>. Francis I made him Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after 1515, and he led the vanguard to victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a>, then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> until May 1516.</p><p>In 1521 he was denied the command of the vanguard that was his by right, and Suzanne died that year. She had willed him her inheritance, but Francis and his mother, Louise, disputed it. In July 1523 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and in August all the disputed lands were sequestered from him. He fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and joined the imperial commanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>He led an invasion of Provence that stalled before Marseille, and he fought at Pavia, where the French king was taken prisoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> hoped to marry the emperor's sister Eleanor, recover his French lands as a sovereign lord and become count of Provence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 1527 his army, unpaid, mutinied in March and ransacked his pavilion, and he advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with no artillery to speak of. Assaults on the city began on the morning of 6 May 1527, in fog. While climbing the walls in white, he was hit by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a> bullet, supposedly fired by the sculptor Benvenuto Cellini. The Prince of Orange succeeded him as head of the army.</p><p>By the end of the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was open to the imperial troops, who sacked it for eight days: thousands were murdered, many raped, and the city pillaged and burned by soldiers angry at their lack of pay. Cloulas estimates the loot at 1,000,000 ducats. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> fled into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a> and capitulated on 6 June, remaining a prisoner until November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles III, Duke of Bourbon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Francis I confiscated the lands of <b>Constable Charles of Bourbon</b>, Forez was united with the Kingdom of France in 1531.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forez">County of Forez</a></li><li><blockquote>It passed to the Bourbons, the Armagnacs and back to the Bourbons as an appanage, until Francis I confiscated the lands of <b>Constable Charles of Bourbon</b> in 1523.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-la-marche">County of La Marche</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles III</b>, constable of France, fell out with Francis I, who claimed his lands; he joined Charles V in 1523 and was killed leading the sack of Rome in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bourbon">Duchy of Bourbon</a></li><li><blockquote>After the confiscation of the <b>Constable of Bourbon's</b> lands in 1523 it was held by the crown, then restored in 1560 to the Bourbon-Montpensier family, who ruled it as a sovereign principality outside the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-dombes">Principality of Dombes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montpensier, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.04,3.22&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Montpensier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4626 543 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles III, Duke of Bourbon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5076 813h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5076 813h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.04,3.22&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
